  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why are residual solvents and elemental impurities controlled separately?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Residual solvents and elemental impurities are not related to the drug molecule. Solvents remain from the manufacturing process, and elements come from catalysts, reagents, water, equipment or packaging. Their risk depends on the toxicity of the solvent or element itself, so each is assessed under its own guideline against a permitted daily exposure, not against the API's impurity criteria."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does a pass on the residual solvents test mean the API contains no solvent?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. A pass means the solvents that were tested are within the acceptance criteria in the specification. It says nothing about solvents that were not on the list. Compare the solvents reported with those used in the route of synthesis, especially in the final steps and in purifying the solid, before treating the result as complete."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why can the same solvent result be acceptable for one product and not another?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Because the acceptable concentration in the material is calculated from a permitted daily exposure and the daily dose of the medicine the material goes into. A higher dose means more of the API, and so more of the solvent, reaches the patient each day. Check the result against your own product's dose, not only against the supplier's specification."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do I need elemental impurity data from my API supplier?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes, if you make a finished product. The elemental impurities guideline is framed around the finished drug product, and the API is one of its contributors alongside excipients, water, equipment and packaging. Without the supplier's risk assessment summary or customer statement, your own assessment has a gap that is hard to close by testing alone."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What does it mean if the certificate still reports a heavy metals test?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "It may mean the specification follows an older edition of the monograph. Several major pharmacopoeias have moved from the non-specific heavy metals test to element-specific methods supported by a risk assessment. Ask the supplier which edition and which guideline the specification follows, and ask for the elemental impurities assessment that should now sit behind it."
      }
    }
  ]
